\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  Peso Ends 1.2% Stronger Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , As Copper Rises\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2011\par 17:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso ended 1.2% stronger against the dollar Wednesday, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ongoing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, as international copper prices jumped.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP490.20 to the dollar, compared with Tuesday's close of CLP496.30, while trading in a range of CLP490.20 to CLP495.10.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's premier copper producer, accounting for nearly a third of global supply, the peso often takes cues from the metal's international prices. Spot copper prices in London jumped 1.5% to $4.36583 per pound, according to Chile's state copper commission Cochilco, after an encouraging Portuguese bond auction bolstered the demand outlook for the metal.\par \par \uc2 Also, the chief executive of Chilean state copper giant Corporacion Nacional del Cobre's expectation that copper will average $4 a pound this year helped to boost the peso.\par \par \uc2 "His forecast likely helped to support the peso's rise," said a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader.\par \par \uc2 With the peso recently trading at 32-month highs against the dollar and exporters demanding help as the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength puts their competitiveness at risk, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to increase its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by $12 billion this year.\par \par \uc2 Before the peso rose Tuesday, it had lost 7.1% against the dollar since Jan. 3, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced the program.\par \par \uc2 During the first phase of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will make daily purchases of $50 million between Jan. 5 and Feb. 9.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million on the local market at an average rate of CLP494.77 to the dollar, on the sixth day of the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program.\par \par \uc2 So far the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has purchased $300 million through its program.\par \par \uc2 Over the coming weeks, traders see the peso trading in a narrow range of CLP490 to CLP500.\par \par \uc2 "Participants are looking for levels to buy dollars, which will bring the exchange rate to around CLP500 in the short to medium term," the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower in light over-the-counter trading.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.80%, from 2.82% on Tuesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 3.19%, from 3.24% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 12-01-11 1708GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110112e71c001af\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
