\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ,  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  Peso Stronger\b0\par\par\uc2 1,271 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2011\par 13:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1203 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's ongoing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  program, the peso was trading stronger against the dollar early Wednesday on the back of a rise in international copper prices and the euro's gains versus the dollar. Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will purchase $50 million in dollars on the local market every day from Jan. 5 through Feb. 9-- the first phase of its program to increase foreign reserves by $12 billion this year, aimed at taming the peso's recent strength. With the peso recently trading at 32-month highs against the dollar, exporters clamored for help as the strength of the peso hurt their competitiveness. Peso recently at CLP493.80 to the dollar, versus CLP496.30 the prior session, according to Valor Futuro newswire. (anthony.esposito@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1140 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro is trading at the day's lows against its peers and a 10-year low against SEK in the wake of the Portuguese auction even though the 10-yr yield came in below the magic 7% print, hyped by many in the market as the make or break level. EUR/USD trades at 1.2963, EUR/JPY 107.86, EUR/CHF 1.2580 and EUR/SEK 8.8477. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1139 GMT [Dow Jones] Portugal 10-year bonds yield 6.855%, up from 6.764% according to Tradeweb, despite a successful auction Wednesday. A trader says that ECB and euro-zone \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying Monday and Tuesday had supported the bonds ahead of the auction, but central banks are not buying Wednesday "so we're just leaking wider after a big rally." (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1138 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro Overnight Index Average fixed lower Tuesday at 0.376% versus Monday's 0.389%. Wednesday, the interbank overnight rate is trading between 0.30% and 0.32%, at the lower end of Tuesday's 0.30% to 0.35% range. This is a result of the high level of surplus liquidity in the money market, traders say, which also led the use of ECB's 0.25% deposit facility to rise Tuesday to its highest level of the year to EUR97.473 billion. Earlier Wednesday, 3-month Euribor fixed unchanged from Tuesday's 0.995% Tuesday. (benjamin.krieger@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1131 GMT [Dow Jones] The Portuguese auction was reasonably successful, but doesn't offer a reason to start buying the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in earnest, says Daragh Maher at Credit Agricole in London. "It's not a game-changer," he says. "It's just one banana skin that we have side-stepped, and we still need to know what the solution is for these economies. That's the big-picture issue. The temptation is always to sell into rallies." EUR/USD at 1.2967, from about 1.30 before the auction results. EUR/CHF also lower at 1.2593 from 1.2650. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1122 GMT [Dow Jones] Financial markets will watch Portugal's underlying deficit to get a better sense of the country's debt sustainability following Monday's auction, says Paolo Pizzoli at ING. Economic growth will play a key role in shaping fiscal adjustment, meaning that the Bank of Portugal's downwards revision of its GDP forecasts for 2011, to a 1.3% contraction "poses the Portuguese government a further presentational problem," because the government based its 2011 budget and the 4.6% deficit-to-GDP target, on a 0.2% GDP growth assumption. The spread of Portugal's 10-year bond over bunds is at 389 bps, up from 381 bps marked at 1035 GMT. (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1117 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro is falling despite the successful Portugal auctions. A UK bank trader says Portuguese yields have risen since the results so it looks like the primary traders are already offloading paper they were 'prodded' to buy. Adds although the yield at the 10-year auction was below 7%, it was close and still very expensive. EUR/USD is now at 1.2982, EUR/CHF at the day's low of 1.2587. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1115 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are off lows with the Portuguese supply now out of the way. Portuguese yields have nudged up, possibly as market makers shed some of the auction stock. The auction results were solid though, with Portugal selling 10-year bonds at 6.716%, tighter than secondary market levels and less than the 7% level being talked by some market participants. The bid to cover ratio was also robust at 3.2. March bunds are down 0.15 at 125.64 and the 10-year Portuguese/German yield spread is now 6.3bps tighter at 387.3 bps vs 383.3 bps before the auction. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1113 GMT [Dow Jones] The results of Portugal's first bond auction of the year "were not too bad," says Annalisa Piazza at Newedge. Still, cheap valuations may have supported demand. And "the extremely high level of yields remains a major reason of concern as not sustainable for a country that already has to face major fiscal challenges this year." The average yield on the June 2020 bonds was 6.716% and on the Oct. 2014 bonds was 5.396%. (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1109 GMT [Dow Jones] Portugal's bond auction brought good over-bidding and decent bid-to-cover ratios, says Ciaran O'Hagan at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . But "the real litmus test" for peripheral euro-zone sovereign bond markets will be Spanish and Portuguese syndicated deals due in the coming weeks. Portugal sold EUR599 million of its June 2020 bond at an average yield of 6.716%, with a bid-to-cover ratio of 3.2x and EUR650 million of its Oct. 2014 bond at 5.396% with a bid-to-cover of 2.6x. (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1059 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro is steady against the dollar, after a spell of nerves prompted by the results of the much-awaited Portuguese bond auction being published slightly later than usual. The yield on the 10-year bond has come in below 7% at 6.716%. The dollar maintains a softer tone and traders will be watching the Federal Reserves Beige Book numbers, due at 1900 GMT and a $21 billion 10-year Treasury auction later Wednesday. Sterling is off the day's highs after disappointing UK trade balance numbers and as traders claim Asian central banks are selling the pound around the 1.5660 level. (eva.szalay@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1059 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR forward premia is slightly lower after data showed India's industrial output growth slowed sharply to a 20-month low in November, temporarily cooling expectations of aggressive rate hikes by the RBI, says a senior dealer with a privately-run bank. The December premium is at 2.5350 vs 2.57 late Tuesday. "Traders are adjusting positions following the 2.7% rise in November factory output but I don't see real value to it because a 25 basis point rate hike looks pretty imminent," the trader says. He tips the 1-year premia to trade in a 2.55-2.75 band for the rest of the week. Market is widely expecting a 25 bp increase in policy rates at the RBI's Jan. 25 policy review. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com) [ 01-12-11 0703ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110112e71c00083\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
