\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/USD Likely Quiet Ahead of Portuguese Auction\b0\par\par\uc2 1,562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2011\par 21:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD will likely tread water in Asia as investors wait to see how the Portuguese bond auction goes later in the global trading day, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  NZ chief manager Daniel Brdanovic. Pair last at 1.2977. "I just think it is holding ground. The market has been waiting for this and then tomorrow there is Spain and Italy, these are the main focal points." Brdanovic says that if there is a good update of the Portuguese bonds "I think it will be positive and it will push aside some of the market speculation that Portugal needs to take a bailout from the EU and the IMF." Once bond auctions are out of the way, attention will shift to the Fed's Beige Book, ECB, BOE rate announcements and a raft of U.S. data including CPI and retail sales. Brdanovic tips resistance Wednesday at 1.2990-1.3000 and support around 1.2905-1.2915. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2026 GMT [Dow Jones] Agency mortgages widened late afternoon after Tsys sold off, post-auction. The 5-10-year blend had been as tight as 145bps but is now quoted at 148 bps, said Paul Jacob of Banc of Manhattan Capital. "Extension risk remains the primary concern," he said.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \par  \par    By Stephen Cox, CMT \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Most of my stock market projections are validated or invalidated in good season, and I regroup and soldier on, or else rejoice, as is appropriate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But in the case of my latest foray, I'm not getting much satisfaction. This is one slow uptrend or I'm simply over-anticipating.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 My latest calls for interim tops of the major U.S. indexes look reasonable, but the slow bull market is teasing out this little human drama.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This column has for some time now been saying that the S&P 500 might find an interim top at 1278.73. The current high for the uptrend is the Thursday high of 1278.17. That's fair marksmanship.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But I added in my original projection that the index, having tested 1278.73, would reverse to the downside and test support at 1241.34. The signal for that lower move would be decisive trading below 1273.77.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of the writing the S&P is trading at 1269.85. You, gentle reader, will have to be the judge of "decisive" in this case.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 My target for the Dow Jones Industrial Average is 11730.20. The current bull market high is Wednesday's high of 11742.70. I don't yet have a satisfying downside target in case the average sells off decisively. In any case, the average is worth watching because an extension of its uptrend, if it comes to that, may be targeting 11968.80. In that case I believe that buyers should consider taking profits at 11854.00.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 My corresponding upside target for the Nasdaq Composite has been the 2746.2-2750.6 band but the bull market high is Tuesday's current high of 2722.70.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It goes without saying - and so I'll say it - if the Nasdaq reaches up toward 2746.2-2750.6 resistance then it might pull the other indexes above the resistance levels I've been stipulating.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Stephen Cox, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2212; stephen.cox@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Stephen Cox, a chartered market technician, is chief technician for Dow Jones Newswires.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Data by CQG)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2021 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro gained only slightly versus the dollar Tuesday, trading in a narrow range as investors remained wary of the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ahead of Portugal's debt auction Wednesday. The euro was unable to maintain momentum following supportive remarks from Japanese and European leaders. Portugal is scheduled to auction up to EUR1.25 billion ($1.61 billion) worth of debt Wednesday. The auction is seen as the next litmus test for investor appetite for European debt during a period of uncertainty. The euro is at $1.2964 in Tuesday afternoon trading in New York, from $1.2952 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.26 compared with Y82.71.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] Changes in offshore risk appetite could drag NZD/USD lower, with global markets firmly focused on signs of deterioration in European sovereign debt markets, says BNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Mike Jones. Pair last at 0.7604. "Poor results from this week's slew of European sovereign bond auctions could knock back risk appetite and the EUR further." Jones says such a scenario may well see NZD/USD test support towards 0.7480. Notes weaker AUD/USD also weighing on Kiwi as the floods in Queensland cause markets to pare back the extent of RBA tightening expected this year. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2020 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P could upgrade Colombia's foreign long-term debt rating from BB+ to BBB- in the future, said S&P sovereign ratings head Joydeep Mukherji in a conference call. "We're signaling that Colombia potentially could be the next sovereign in Latin America to join the investment grade category," he said. Crucial factors in boosting the rating include GDP growth rates that demonstrate a solid economy, controlled government spending, and a fiscal situation that reflects a booming energy sector, the analyst said. "Crossing from BB+ to BBB- is a sticky passage," he added, so S&P will examine Colombia's ability to withstand stress tests over the next three to five years. (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2016 GMT [Dow Jones] Most traders are focused on getting over the hump of Wednesday's Portuguese debt auction, but Spain and Italy are also due to sell securities this week. Analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Western Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  say this makes for a mix that will see the euro "spike" in volatility. "Amid the uncertainty, many traders with large short positions will trim their exposures, while others will seek to place new bets on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's direction--leading to choppy trading conditions. Sharp reversals are common in this sort of environment, making it a good time to consider placing limit orders," the firm said in a research note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (javier.david@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2013 GMT [Dow Jones] Sovereign credit default swaps on Slovenia are 6.58BP wider, or 7.6% worse, Tuesday, according to CMA DataVision. The country sold EUR1.5 billion in new 10-year bonds Monday, at 125 bps over midswaps, according to a person familiar with the deal. The deal was reportedly oversubscribed, but jitters about the amount of supply coming from other European nations issuing debt may have unnerved investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (katy.burne@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1955 GMT [Dow Jones] The front end of the government of Canada curve is vulnerable to a sell-off on growing evidence of acceleration of Canadian economic growth, says Bank of America/Merrill Lynch. "As a result, we continue to favor calendar steepeners and outright short positions in the 2-year note, with a target of 100bp for the 2-year/ overnight curve (vs spot 77bp)," it says. "The long end of the curve continues to be dominated by foreign investor purchases," Merrill adds. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0024 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD to track any moves in AUD, with Australian climatic conditions dominating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  direction, says ANZ Senior Dealer Alex Sinton. ANZ economists estimate the floods would shave at least 0.5% off 1Q GDP, with a net loss of 0.3% of GDP over 2011. Pair last at 0.7592 vs 0.7587 late Tuesday. Says any topside levels above 0.7650 remain out of reach until such time as markets fully price the impact of the Queensland flooding. Sinton tips a range of 0.7565-0.7635 short term. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1916 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  unit Banamex has raised its economic growth estimate for Mexico in 2011 to 4.8% from 3.9%, citing expectations of higher growth in the U.S. As a result, Banamex expects consumer price inflation of 3.9%, up from it previous estimate of 3.5%, and expects that the Bank of Mexico will start raising its overnight rate from the current 4.5% in October, instead of waiting until 2012. Banamex sees the overnight rate reaching 5% by year-end. (Anthony.harrup@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1912 GMT [Dow Jones] Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely hike its benchmark interest rate on Thursday by 25 basis points to 3.5%, since Chile's peso has been responsive to the government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts and economic growth remains robust, Bulltick Research said in a note. Still, the peso's decline may be short-lived, especially in light of pressure Tuesday from strong copper prices. "If commodity prices continue to rise this year (5%-10% in our expectation) and Chile posts strong economic growth of 6.0% as we expect, appreciation pressures will continue," pushing USD/CLP to 465," Bulltick said. But \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Chile\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will not follow in Brazil's footsteps with further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  measures like capital restrictions, the note added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-11-11 1552ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110111e71b000dg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
