\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Euro Little Changed Against Dollar As Traders Await Auction\b0\par\par\uc2 1,189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2011\par 21:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Robert Flint, 212-416-2216; \par robert.flint@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2021 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro gained only slightly versus the dollar Tuesday, trading in a narrow range as investors remained wary of the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ahead of Portugal's debt auction Wednesday. The euro was unable to maintain momentum following supportive remarks from Japanese and European leaders. Portugal is scheduled to auction up to EUR1.25 billion ($1.61 billion) worth of debt Wednesday. The auction is seen as the next litmus test for investor appetite for European debt during a period of uncertainty. The euro is at $1.2964 in Tuesday afternoon trading in New York, from $1.2952 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.26 compared with Y82.71.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] Changes in offshore risk appetite could drag NZD/USD lower, with global markets firmly focused on signs of deterioration in European sovereign debt markets, says BNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Mike Jones. Pair last at 0.7604. "Poor results from this week's slew of European sovereign bond auctions could knock back risk appetite and the EUR further." Jones says such a scenario may well see NZD/USD test support towards 0.7480. Notes weaker AUD/USD also weighing on Kiwi as the floods in Queensland cause markets to pare back the extent of RBA tightening expected this year. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2020 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P could upgrade Colombia's foreign long-term debt rating from BB+ to BBB- in the future, said S&P sovereign ratings head Joydeep Mukherji in a conference call. "We're signaling that Colombia potentially could be the next sovereign in Latin America to join the investment grade category," he said. Crucial factors in boosting the rating include GDP growth rates that demonstrate a solid economy, controlled government spending, and a fiscal situation that reflects a booming energy sector, the analyst said. "Crossing from BB+ to BBB- is a sticky passage," he added, so S&P will examine Colombia's ability to withstand stress tests over the next three to five years. (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2016 GMT [Dow Jones] Most traders are focused on getting over the hump of Wednesday's Portuguese debt auction, but Spain and Italy are also due to sell securities this week. Analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Western Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  say this makes for a mix that will see the euro "spike" in volatility. "Amid the uncertainty, many traders with large short positions will trim their exposures, while others will seek to place new bets on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's direction--leading to choppy trading conditions. Sharp reversals are common in this sort of environment, making it a good time to consider placing limit orders," the firm said in a research note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (javier.david@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2013 GMT [Dow Jones] Sovereign credit default swaps on Slovenia are 6.58BP wider, or 7.6% worse, Tuesday, according to CMA DataVision. The country sold EUR1.5 billion in new 10-year bonds Monday, at 125 bps over midswaps, according to a person familiar with the deal. The deal was reportedly oversubscribed, but jitters about the amount of supply coming from other European nations issuing debt may have unnerved investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (katy.burne@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1955 GMT [Dow Jones] The front e