\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  Peso Weaker, Nears CLP500 On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,140 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2011\par 13:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1204 GMT [Dow Jones] Chile's peso is trading weaker versus the dollar early Monday, approaching the CLP500 level, which it hasn't traded at since early September. The peso has lost some 7% versus the dollar since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced last Monday a $12 billion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  program. The program, which looks to tame the peso's recent strength, started with daily purchases of $50 million from January 5 through February 9. In recent trading the peso was weaker at CLP499.00 to the dollar, versus Friday's close of CLP497.60, according to data provided by local newswire Valor Futuro. (anthony.esposito@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD has started to turn down again after printing the day's high of 1.2939 in the wake of wire headlines that Germany is not pressing Portugal to accept financial aid. EUR/USD now trades at 1.2912, still some way above the day's near four-month lows of 1.2860, which came in early trade and had a lot to do with an article in German newspaper Der Spiegel that said Germany, France and others were pressing Portugal to accept outside help. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1152 GMT [Dow Jones] The speed of the recent appreciation of the franc versus the euro is the main problem posed to Swiss exporters, rather than the actual level, says Economiesuisse. The franc has appreciated steadily in recent years, resulting in innovation and cost efficiencies among Swiss exporting countries, but the recent gains have simply been too rapid, according to Economiesuisse vice-president Patrick Odier. EUR/CHF at 1.2464. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1152 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD Index is ready to add to the 3% gains already seen this year, and a push above 81.444 would create room for further gains towards 84.588 and 88.000. See Monday's Charting Europe column by Francis Bray. (francis.bray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1136 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/GBP's bounce from near 4 month lows Monday looks more a case of position adjustments rather than a reaction to the poor Halifax December housing numbers says Citi. The bank saw short-term models buying back the cross near the lows, but says it has since seen good selling interest around the 0.8330 level. EUR/GBP now trades at 0.8328 within the day's range of 0.8285-0.8339. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1133 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro Overnight Index Average fixed a tic higher Friday at 0.380% versus Thursday's 0.379%. Monday, the interbank overnight rate is trading between 0.28% and 0.38%, a wider range from Friday's 0.32% and 0.37%. Markets remain calm and traders expects rates to remain at these levels in the coming days. Meanwhile, the use of ECB's 0.25% deposit facility rose to EUR80.966 billion over the weekend. The funds in the deposit facility, combined with a EUR20 billion overhang of reserves in banks' current accounts, imply that banks are running a total surplus of liquidity in the region of EUR101 billion. Earlier Monday, 3-month Euribor fixed at 0.995% vs 0.997% Friday. (herbert.rude@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1132 GMT [Dow Jones] Instead of being one of the ugly sisters, the USD is starting to look a little more like Cinderella, says Nicholas Hastings in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus. Not only has it shrugged off disappointing US employment data but it has also largely ignored the latest warnings from China that the USD portion of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves is too high. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1125 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swiss government's planned round-table talks with banking, industry and trade union representatives Friday to discuss the strong franc is unlikely to come out with proposals to peg the franc to the euro, says Economiesuisse. "An official pegging of the franc to the euro is unrealistic, and would cost the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  billions of francs," says Economiesuisse head Gerold Buehrer, who is due to attend the talks. "There is no wonder weapon against the franc's appreciation versus the euro," he notes. "The weak euro is something the EU has to solve, and we remain skeptical on a quick solution of the problem." EUR/CHF at 1.2468. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1118 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD is heading back toward the day's high of 1.2930 after printing a near four-month low of 1.2860 in early trade. Amid talk of sovereign demand below 1.29 and aggressive buying of Portuguese and Irish paper by the ECB, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has almost wiped out the session losses against its peers while non-core euro zone yield spreads have tightened. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1113 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD index may currently be trapped between its 55 and 200DMA's at 79.13 and 81.69 but \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says the latter seems the most exposed. A daily close above 81.69 should trigger another up-leg toward 83.52-71 which is the breakpoint to 85.90 and the 4 year resistance line at 88.21 says the bank. Now at 81.12 \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says only below 78.36 will it re-evaluate its positive take on the index. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1107 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  expects the German Finance Agency to set a 2% coupon on a new series of five-year federal note, or bobl, Tuesday, says strategist David Schnautz. The Finance Agency will launch the new bobl line, maturing February 2016, with EUR6 billion at an auction Wednesday, a day after it has fixed the coupon. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1106 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: India's November industrial output growth likely slowed to 6.0% on-year from 10.8% in October, according to the median estimate in a Dow Jones poll of 15 economists. The data is due at 0530 GMT Wednesday. The likely slower growth is attributed to a sharp pullback in infrastructure output growth, while factories also scaled back production to clear inventories piled up during October festivities. Anjali Verma, an economist at \uc2 MF Global\uc2  India, says production had increased sharply to meet October's festive demand, therefore some moderation in output to clear out the remaining stocks is inevitable. She expects industrial output growth at 7.2%. Growth in infrastructure output, which has a 26.7% weight in the industrial production index, also slowed sharply to 2.3% on-year in November, compared with 7.0% in October, government data showed. (anant.kala@dowjones.com) [ 01-10-11 0705ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110110e71a00087\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
