\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Buys $50 Mln, 3rd Day Of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,118 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2011\par 16:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Robert Flint, 212-416-2216; \par robert.flint@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1545 GMT [Dow Jones] The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Chile\f28 \uc2 \f2  purchased $50 million in dollars on the local market at an average rate of CLP497.03 to the dollar, the third time the bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since it started on Wednesday a program to increase foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves by $12 billion this year. With the peso recently trading at 32-month highs against the dollar and exporters clamoring for help as the strength of the peso hurt their competitiveness, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moved to tame the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's appreciation. During the first phase of the program, the bank will make daily purchases of $50 million between Jan 5 and Feb 9. Late in the session, USD/CLP was at 497.50, versus Thursday's close at 495.60, according to data provided by local newswire Valor Futuro. (anthony.esposito@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1545 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira and bonds are weaker as EUR loses ground against USD. USD/TRY trades at 1.5665, from 1.5460 Thursday. Meanwhile the benchmark August 8, 2012 bond ends trades at 6.99% from 6.87% Thursday, but it moves to 6.93% in Monday value-dated transactions. Analysts forecast TRY will trade between 1.5360 and 1.5700, and the benchmark bond between 6.83% and 7.05%, in the short term.\par \par \uc2 1545 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed's Bernanke says the dollar 'quite stable' when addressing a Senator's question on the price of oil and its effects on the US economy. Bernanke says the Fed pays attention to energy and other commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (bradley.davis@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1538 GMT [Dow Jones] Emerging-market debt slightly weaker versus Treasurys, showing little response to US jobs data. The risk premium on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Global is three basis points wider to 275 basis points over Treasurys. The index is down 0.18% in price terms. RBC analysts note that EM asset performance has been mixed in the last couple weeks and that the primary concern holding investors back is uncertainty over how the euro-zone sovereign periphery crisis may unfold. (anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1537 GMT [Dow Jones] Nearest 1-yr calendar spread even tighter as Eurodollar futures traders view Bernanke's comments and disappointing jobs data as signs short-term rates won't be rising much anytime soon. Mar 2012 contract recently prices in rate 66.5 BP higher than rate priced into Mar '11 contract. Same spread at 69 BP just before release of Bernanke's remarks, and at 74 BP at Thurs' settlement. Price for quarterly 2011 Eurodollar contracts recently up 1.5 to 7.5 BP. 2012 contracts recently up as much as 12 BP. (Howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1535 GMT [Dow Jones] Munis have a slightly weaker tone, with 10-yr bonds appearing to see the most pressure, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Miller Tabak\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Michael Pietronico. "There's a fair amount of selling for a Friday," he says. "Probably a lot has to do with positioning post payrolls." Still, trading is thin, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth Mgmt's Gary Pollack estimates 10-yr bonds just a bp or so weaker thus far. "I think there's a lot of fear in the muni market," Pollack adds. Mutual funds continue to lose money, he added, and next week's calendar is much healthier, with an estimated $3.4B in negotiated sales, according to Ipreo LLC.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (kelly.nolan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1533 GMT [Dow Jones] RBC analysts note that within emerging markets there is clearly endorsement building among policymakers on more market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or protectionist policies, as seen in Brazil, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Chile\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Israel this week. The analysts believe this trend will only deepen in the short term, which could inject higher volatility and downward pressures on EM \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and EM local financial assets, bonds and equities, in particular. (anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1530 GMT [Dow Jones] Growth in the ECRI weekly leading index strengthened further in latest week. The growth rate for the week of December 31 improved to 3.3% from 2.3%, but the index itself slipped to 128.9 from 129.0. Growth in the December index strengthened to 2.3% from -2.3% in November. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1525 GMT [Dow Jones] Bernanke and payrolls report galvanize Treasury steepening yield curve trade. Some investors buy short-dated notes and sell long-dated bonds as the latter bears the brunt of next week's new debt sales, traders say. With Benernake still signaling a slow recovery, interest rates futures cut bets on a hike in fed-funds target rate by end of 2011 and that gives the two-year Treasury a boost because the yield is the most sensitive to changes in Fed rate outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1524 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  topped the list of bookrunners for US asset-backed deals valued at $23.1 mln in 2010, according to Asset-Backed Alert, a trade publication. \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  took the next two slots. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  was also the top manager for ABS, with 79 deals valued at $62.29 mln. RBS and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  took the second and third slots.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1524 GMT [Dow Jones] March bunds are slightly higher, having flipped between positive and negative territory as traders try and make sense of the nonfarm payrolls report. While the headline nonfarm payrolls number fell short of estimates (+103k vs +150k forecast), the unemployment rate declined to 9.4%, the lowest level since May 2009. Bunds also got support from widening yield spreads on peripheral euro zone government bonds and non-core borrowers may stay under pressure ahead of bond supply next week, with Portugal, Italy, and Spain all tapping the market. March bunds are up 0.12 at 125.85 and the 10-year bund is little changed at 96.54, yielding 2.904%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1520 GMT [Dow Jones] The ADP private-sector wmployment data's miss on Dec NFP today marks the "death knell" for the ADP report and the final tolerance for using it as a preliminary guide to nonfarm payroll expectations, says Scotia Capital. "This is the worst performance that ADP has put in by way of a preliminary first-pass guide to nonfarm," Scotia says. Despite ADP's guidance that private payroll jobs rose almost 300K in Dec, nonfarm private payrolls rose only 113K, Scotia says. (don.curren@dowjones.com) [ 01-07-11 1045ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110107e717000at\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
