a\uc2  took the next two slots. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  was also the top manager for ABS, with 79 deals valued at $62.29 mln. RBS and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  took the second and third slots.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1524 GMT [Dow Jones] March bunds are slightly higher, having flipped between positive and negative territory as traders try and make sense of the nonfarm payrolls report. While the headline nonfarm payrolls number fell short of estimates (+103k vs +150k forecast), the unemployment rate declined to 9.4%, the lowest level since May 2009. Bunds also got support from widening yield spreads on peripheral euro zone government bonds and non-core borrowers may stay under pressure ahead of bond supply next week, with Portugal, Italy, and Spain all tapping the market. March bunds are up 0.12 at 125.85 and the 10-year bund is little changed at 96.54, yielding 2.904%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1520 GMT [Dow Jones] The ADP private-sector wmployment data's miss on Dec NFP today marks the "death knell" for the ADP report and the final tolerance for using it as a preliminary guide to nonfarm payroll expectations, says Scotia Capital. "This is the worst performance that ADP has put in by way of a preliminary first-pass guide to nonfarm," Scotia says. Despite ADP's guidance that private payroll jobs rose almost 300K in Dec, nonfarm private payrolls rose only 113K, Scotia says. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1512 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY erases gains on day and declines slightly after limp jobs data. Blame that on QE2 expectations, says Steven Englander of Citi. "The markets now think it's likely that the Fed will go all the way on QE," says Englander. After this data, forget about the outside chance of ending the $600 billion asset-purchase program prematurely, he says. USD/JPY at 83.15 from 83.69 before data and 83.36 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1510 GMT [Dow Jones] Sell USD/JPY at 83.26 with a stop on a daily close above 84.34 and a take profit level at 80.23 says Lloyds Bank Corporate Markets. The bank says the reason is that the rates long-term decline is resuming after failing at major trend resistance and a short-term sell signal has just been completed. USD/JPY now at 83.13 from the day's high of 83.70. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1509 GMT [Dow Jones] Mexico's peso is slightly stronger against the dollar following the U.S. December payrolls disappointment, with USD/MXN recently at 12.2170, according to CQG, compared with 12.2199 Thursday when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was hit by a broad rally in the dollar. On the local front, the Bank of Mexico is set to report inflation for December. An expected 0.39% rise in the CPI would bring full-year 2010 inflation to 4.29%--within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's forecast range for the fourth quarter. (anthony.harrup@dowjones.com) [ 01-07-11 1039ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110107e717000aq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
