\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  Could Cause More Volatility In EM - RBC\b0\par\par\uc2 1,094 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2011\par 16:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Robert Flint, 212-416-2216; \par robert.flint@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1533 GMT [Dow Jones] RBC analysts note that within emerging markets there is clearly endorsement building among policymakers on more market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or protectionist policies, as seen in Brazil, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Chile\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Israel this week. The analysts believe this trend will only deepen in the short term, which could inject higher volatility and downward pressures on EM \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and EM local financial assets, bonds and equities, in particular. (anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1530 GMT [Dow Jones] Growth in the ECRI weekly leading index strengthened further in latest week. The growth rate for the week of December 31 improved to 3.3% from 2.3%, but the index itself slipped to 128.9 from 129.0. Growth in the December index strengthened to 2.3% from -2.3% in November. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1525 GMT [Dow Jones] Bernanke and payrolls report galvanize Treasury steepening yield curve trade. Some investors buy short-dated notes and sell long-dated bonds as the latter bears the brunt of next week's new debt sales, traders say. With Benernake still signaling a slow recovery, interest rates futures cut bets on a hike in fed-funds target rate by end of 2011 and that gives the two-year Treasury a boost because the yield is the most sensitive to changes in Fed rate outlook.\par \par \uc2 (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1524 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays\uc2  topped the list of bookrunners for US asset-backed deals valued at $23.1 mln in 2010, according to Asset-Backed Alert, a trade publication. \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  took the next two slots. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  was also the top manager for ABS, with 79 deals valued at $62.29 mln. RBS and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  took the second and third slots.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1524 GMT [Dow Jones] March bunds are slightly higher, having flipped between positive and negative territory as traders try and make sense of the nonfarm payrolls report. While the headline nonfarm payrolls number fell short of estimates (+103k vs +150k forecast), the unemployment rate declined to 9.4%, the lowest level since May 2009. Bunds also got support from widening yield spreads on peripheral euro zone government bonds and non-core borrowers may stay under pressure ahead of bond supply next week, with Portugal, Italy, and Spain all tapping the market. March bunds are up 0.12 at 125.85 and the 10-year bund is little changed at 96.54, yielding 2.904%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1520 GMT [Dow Jones] The ADP private-sector wmployment data's miss on Dec NFP today marks the "death knell" for the ADP report and the final tolerance for using it as a preliminary guide to nonfarm payroll expectations, says Scotia Capital. "This is the worst performance that ADP has put in by way of a preliminary first-pass guide to nonfarm," Scotia says. Despite ADP's guidance that private payroll jobs rose almost 300K in Dec, nonfarm private payrolls rose only 113K, Scotia says. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1512 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY erases gains on day and declines slightly after limp jobs data. Blame that on QE2 expectations, says Steven Englander of Citi. "The markets now think it's likely that the Fed will go all the way on QE," says Englander. After this data, forget about the outside chance of ending the $600 billion asset-purchase program prematurely, he says. USD/JPY at 83.15 from 83.69 before data and 83.36 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1510 GMT [Dow Jones] Sell USD/JPY at 83.26 with a stop on a daily close above 84.34 and a take profit level at 80.23 says Lloyds Bank Corporate Markets. The bank says the reason is that the rates long-term decline is resuming after failing at major trend resistance and a short-term sell signal has just been completed. USD/JPY now at 83.13 from the day's high of 83.70. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1509 GMT [Dow Jones] Mexico's peso is slightly stronger against the dollar following the U.S. December payrolls disappointment, with USD/MXN recently at 12.2170, according to CQG, compared with 12.2199 Thursday when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was hit by a broad rally in the dollar. On the local front, the Bank of Mexico is set to report inflation for December. An expected 0.39% rise in the CPI would bring full-year 2010 inflation to 4.29%--within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's forecast range for the fourth quarter. (anthony.harrup@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1504 GMT [Dow Jones] States are starting to move on improving balance sheets. First New York's Cuomo promises to slash spending in a "crisis" budget. Now, Illinois' Quinn and legislative leaders tentatively agree to raise state's income and cigarette taxes and use some of the funds to borrow $8.5 billion to pay off overdue bills. Meanwhile, Wisconsin's Scott Walker wants to reorganize the state's Commerce Dept as a public-private authority in order to get jobless residents back to work. New York State faces a $10 bln deficit and Illinois $13 million. Also in the wings: Jerry Brown's budget Monday as California swims in $28 bln of red ink over next 18 mos. (stan.rosenberg@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1502 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  now expects the BOE to hike interest rates in 3Q as inflation accelerates, changing its previous call for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stay on hold this year. "The deterioration in inflation and expectations is a sign that the output gap is not as wide as previously assumed," the bank says. Should the inflation outlook worsen further, the BOE could even raise rates before the middle of the year, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1501 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed Chairman Bernanke has offered to provide lawmakers on the US Senate Budget Committee a closed-door chat on his outlook for the economy, the panel's chairman said Friday. Sen. Kent Conrad (D., N.D.) disclosed the offer at the beginning of Bernanke's first Capitol Hill appearance of the new year. The Fed in the last year has increased its outreach to Capitol Hill, eager to assuage lawmakers' concerns about its monetary policy and regulatory efforts. [ 01-07-11 1033ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110107e717000al\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
