\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Buys $50M, 2nd Day Of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2011\par 14:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1319 GMT [Dow Jones] Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought another $50 million in dollars on the local market at an average of CLP493.84 to the dollar, the second time the bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since announcing late Monday its program to increase foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves by $12 billion this year. With the peso trading at 32-month highs against the dollar recently, exporters clamored for help as the strength of the peso hurt their competitiveness. In the first phase of the program, the bank will make daily purchases of $50 million on the local market between Jan. 5 and ending Feb. 9. After losing over 6% the prior two sessions, the peso was recently mildly stronger at CLP494.20 to the dollar, versus Wednesday's close of CLP494.80, according to data provided by local newswire Valor Futuro. (anthony.esposito@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1310 GMT [Dow Jones] Ireland will have to pay 5.51% on the loan it is getting from the European Union under its rescue package agreed in December, according to the European Commission. That will comprise the 2.59% it will cost the EU to borrow funds, plus a margin of 2.925% that will be distributed back to all 27 member states. Ireland will get its funding Jan. 12. The EU sold EUR5 billion of five-year bonds Wednesday to finance the first tranche of the EU/IMF support for Ireland. The bonds sold at 12 bp over midswaps, at the tight end of price guidance. (katy.burne@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1310 GMT [Dow Jones] A positive short-term rally in USD/JPY should not be mistaken for a reversal of the dominant bear trend. With bears gathered in the 85.25 area, fresh pressure towards last year's low at 80.21 and the 79.75 all-time low is expected, with scope to 75.00 for 1H 2011. See Thursday's Charting Europe column by Francis Bray. (francis.bray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1308 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD is down as the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fails to respond to positive euro-zone data, including an unexpected jump in a German manufacturing index. Investors are focused on emcouraging signs of US recovery in contrast with never-ending worries over euro-zone sovereign debt. EUR/USD at 1.3093 from 1.3150 late Wed, according to EBS via CQG. US weekly jobless claims, due at 1400 GMT (8:30 am ET), will be watched for signs of whether the US economy continues to outperform, analysts say. The most crucial data comes with Friday non-farm payrolls, a positive Thursday number could lead to further USD gains. USD/JPY slightly down, at 83.17 from 83.25.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (bradley.davis@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1307 GMT [Dow Jones] The chances of a rate hike in Poland are rising, which is good news for the zloty, but risks still remain, says Lutz Karpowitz of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The rising inflation rate makes a rate hike in Poland increasingly likely," he says, adding recent hawkish comments from key MPC members mean rate hikes could be on the cards. "We therefore stick to our positive view of the zloty and expect to see EUR/PLN at 3.60 by the end of 2011," he says, noting most of this appreciation should occur during the next few months. EUR/PLN currently trades at 3.8569. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1306 GMT [Dow Jones] New measures from Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are unlikely to put any extra pressure on the local spot dollar exchange rate, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Measures, designed to boost local demand for dollars by upping banks' reserve requirements for short USD positions, will cause tighter trading on BM&FBovespa futures contracts, RBC says. Impact could also extend to short-dated forwards, it says. Real, meanwhile, is regaining strength mid-morning to BRL1.6794 vs USD after dipping to BRL1.6842 as measures were announced. (diana.kinch@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1303 GMT [Dow Jones] Could Brazil be in the midst of an important shift in economic policy? Measures unveiled by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Thursday to try to halt the appreciation of the real suggest the government may oppose any rise in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's benchmark rate, the Selic, to control prices, and will use other means to control inflation (higher reserve requirements, fiscal tightening), says Andre Guilherme, of Gradual Investimentos. "If that's the objective we're witnessing an important change in the country's economic policy. The government will never admit that, but it's exactly what's being communicated to the market." (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1302 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD drops to 1.3087 as US trading gets underway. The area between 1.3080 and 1.3085 is seen as a crucial support, as that's where the 200-day moving average lies. Chart-watching technical strategists say a break below there could trigger a drop to the 1.2970 low from late November. Now 1.3092. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1241 GMT [Dow Jones] Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has encouraged Brazilian banks to increase short-dollar positions to a "record high" $16.8 billion because of its "aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy (soaking up excess onshore dollar liquidity)," says RBS Securities. Banks are encouraged to borrow dollars offshore and then buy reais, which offers much higher returns, it says. "If we think that this measure aims to reduce this arbitrage opportunity, then it may be possible that this is also an indication of less \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , at least not in excess of net daily flows," says RBS. Impact on the offshore market is limited at this stage given this measure impacts locals, it says. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1235 GMT [Dow Jones] SovX Western Europe is still in positive territory, being 0.25 bps tighter from the close at 201.25 bps, having been even wider at the open, Thursday. Belgium is still the underperformer, having widened 1.75 bps to 220/229 bps, according to Markit. However, trading is light in the name and the move, on continuing political uncertainty, is "marginal," a trader says. Overall "the [sovereign CDS] market hasn't really found a trend and is very range-bound so far this year. We're waiting for more headlines, and for peripheral supply." (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1233 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD is holding its overnight gains and focus will now turn to the 1330GMT release of U.S. weekly jobless claims says \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . After Wednesday's ADP surprise the bank says the claims figure has some downside risks which could give the dollar more upside and add to the argument that the greenback is becoming a growth \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rather than a safe haven. The DJN forecast is for a 410k print, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  says any number below 400k should result in more dollar buying. EUR/USD now at 1.3115, USD/JPY at 83.15. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ("MARKET TALK: Sterling, Euro Under Pressure," at 1054 GMT, misstated, in the second sentence, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against which the euro was selling off. The correct version follows:)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1054 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets are relatively subdued in mid-morning European trading hours Thursday with no clear trend emerging as caution sets in ahead of Friday's key US nonfarm payrolls report, which is suddenly expected to be exceptionally strong after the upbeat ADP labor report Wednesday. In an otherwise dull session, sterling took centre stage after December services PMI severely disappointed, causing a sell-off in the pound against the euro and the dollar. Meanwhile, the dollar and euro were both mixed as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets treaded water ahead of the key report. The euro is toying with a serious break below $1.31, which chart-watchers see as a green light for a heavy fall. USD/JPY is little changed at 83.12, while GBP/USD is down at 1.5480.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com) [ 01-06-11 0819ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110106e7160008c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
