\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Chile, Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  slump; Mexico gains\b0\par\par\uc2 636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2011\par 14:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Chile's peso falls 1.5 pct on first day of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real dips 0.66 pct, Mexican peso gains 0.28 pct\par \par \uc2 * Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  near 8-week high on U.S. optimism (Adds comments, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Samantha Pearson and Brad Haynes\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO/SANTIAGO, Jan 5 (Reuters) - Chile's peso and Brazil's real weakened on Wednesday as investors fretted over official meddling in the countries' markets.\par \par \uc2 But Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is not seen to be at risk from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , firmed to just below a eight-week high touched in the previous session on encouraging U.S. labor data.\par \par \uc2 Chile's market was still reeling from Monday's news that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will buy $50 million daily to curb the peso's rise -- a program that kicks off on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 After suffering its biggest one-day loss in more than 25 years in the previous session, the Chilean peso <CLP=> weakened 1.5 percent more to a three-month low of 495 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The effect of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be magnified as the price of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main export -- copper -- slips from record highs, but analysts are not expecting another drop as dramatic as Tuesday's plunge. For details, see [ID:nN05260728]\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain rangebound with a bias to appreciate for the coming months," said Bertrand Delgado, an economist at Roubini Global Economics in New York, who sees the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading between 480 and 510 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The (Chilean) economy will continue to grow above potential and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to keep hiking interest rates, so it is still positive story, but now there is the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Delgado said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso had surged nearly 18 percent in the second half of 2010, as investors were drawn to the country's strong local economy and rising interest rates. The peso hit a nearly three-year high before the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced its dollar buying program on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, investors in the Brazilian market were cautious after the government issued fresh warnings on Tuesday that it was ready to take tougher action if the real firms further.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real <BRBY> bid 0.66 percent weaker at 1.673 per U.S. dollar on the local spot market.\par \par \uc2 If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to weaken, traders said it is likely to find strong technical support at 1.70 per dollar -- a psychologically- and technically-important level.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian "Finance Minister (Guido) Mantega keeps repeating his mantra that the government has tools to prevent excessive appreciation, including further measures to restrain capital flows," Zeina Latif, chief Brazil economist at RBS in Sao Paulo, said in a research note. "But the point is that there might be few options remaining without crossing over to more heterodox measures."\par \par \uc2 "We should expect more verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with (the real) below 1.65, but not meaningful \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures," she added.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso <MXN=> firmed 0.28 percent to 12.1910 per dollar after a much stronger-than-expected rise in new private jobs in December in the United States, Mexico's top trading partner. [ID:nN05266445]\par \par \uc2 Highlighting Mexican authorities' lack of concern about a significantly stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the country's finance minister said on Wednesday that the peso would be stable in 2011, likely trading between 12.17 and 12.70 per dollar. [ID:nN05269953]\par \par \uc2 Analysts at some banks see the Mexican peso breaking past the 12 per dollar per level this year.\par \par \uc2 But even with such gains, the peso remains below the levels it traded at before the 2008 financial crisis, easing concerns that officials could step up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  any time soon.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110108e71500072\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
