\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Turkey, Philippines Tap Bond Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 1,187 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2011\par 21:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Robert Flint, 212-416-2216; \par robert.flint@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2039 GMT [Dow Jones] The governments of the Philippines and Turkey tapped global credit markets Wednesday, launching large global bond issues in an effort to tap strong investor demand for emerging world assets. The Philippines sold a $1.25 billion bond issue denominated in pesos and payable in dollars at a yield of 6.25%, according to people familiar with the deal. The Philippines bond issue was priced at par. Meanwhile, Turkey launched a $1 billion 30-year bond at a yield of 6.25%, according to a person close to the Turkish deal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2033 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/USD is weaker after U.S. data boosted belief in the U.S. economic recovery but it may retrace losses as it has already come off sharply, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  NZ chief manager Daniel Brdanovic. "A strong ADP employment number came out overnight and you've had treasury yields up 10-15 bp across the curve so there has been U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength." The AUD/USD is at 1.0002 vs 1.0007 late Wednesday in Australia. Brdanovic adds Australian November building approvals are due later but market is more focused on the U.S. nonfarms payroll number for December due Friday for further direction. He puts support at 0.9960 with resistance at 1.0110. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2027 GMT [Dow Jones] With no large asset-backed deals announced so far this week, it is unlikely issuers will tap the market until next week. Industry participants expect issuance to be in the $100B range this year, with auto sector bonds dominating the list like the past two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2002 GMT [Dow Jones] Agency mortgages have improved somewhat from their earlier losses, but still remain wider on the day, as they continue to track the rise in Treasury yields. Buyers came in the afternoon attracted by the lower prices. Risk premiums are 2 bps wider on the day at 140 bps over comparable Treasury yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2000 GMT [Dow Jones]EUR/USD is weaker after the USD strengthened against most major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  overnight as upbeat economic data stoked optimism about the U.S. recovery, says BNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Mike Jones; "A mild flaring of European sovereign debt concerns also provided headwinds for the EUR." The pair was last at 1.3140. He adds whether or not the USD can extend its recent gains will depend on Friday's non-farm payrolls data and comments from the Fed's Bernanke. He tips support at 1.3080 and resistance at 1.3185. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1947 GMT [Dow Jones] Brazil's real slipped Wednesday to end weaker against the dollar as lower commodities prices abroad and government threats of market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  helped push the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  away from recent highs. USD/BRL ended at 1.6715 from 1.6610 Tuesday, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telekurs\f28 \uc2 \f2  via Factset. The real failed to rally as lower commodity prices abroad set the tone for the session. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was also restrained by declarations by Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega late Tuesday that Brazil's government had "infinite" measures at its disposal to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in case a strong local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  threatened to hurt the country's trade balance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1922 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys are getting whacked across the board with most yields notching double-digit rise. ADP report fuels the selloff as investors turns to stocks and commodities. The benchmark 10-yr yield recently hits a session high of 3.469%, not far from the 3.568% set on Dec 16, the highest since May. In recent trade, the benchmark 10-year note is 31/32 lower to yield 3.460%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1919 GMT [Dow Jones] Total state government revenue dropped to $1.1 trillion in 2009, a decline of 31% from $1.6 trillion in 2008, according to the U.S. Census Bureau. Tax income dropped by 8.5%, the first year-to-year decline in tax revenue since 2002. Meanwhile, state government spending rose by 3%. Total federal grants to states increased 13% to $477.7B.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (romy.varghese@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1918 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is a touch weaker after USD got a further boost overnight and further moves lower look likely today, says ANZ Senior Dealer Alex Sinton; "Support will hold on any deeper moves into the 0.7500 territory during local time as direction is guided by the Australian Dollar moves." The pair was last trading at 0.7609 vs 0.7637 late Wednesday. Sinton adds the USD moved higher overnight after the U.S. December Automated Data Payroll release forecasted a very strong U.S. December non-farm payroll release on Friday. Sinton expects a range of 0.7587 to 0.7639. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1916 GMT [Dow Jones] President Obama will press Chinese President Hu Jintao on Beijing's policy on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when the two meet at the White House in two weeks, White House spokesman Robert Gibbs said. The US continues to believe that China must take steps to allow its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the yuan, to continue to appreciate, said Gibbs. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy is a long-standing bone of contention between the two countries. The US views the yuan as artifically undervalued, while China is reluctant to loosen its hold on the yuan for fear of costing millions of Chinese workers their jobs because a stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has made the country's export products too expensive in world markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (michael.crittenden@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1853 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar gained broadly against its rivals after encouraging US data painted a brightening picture of the economy. The dollar soared to a two-week high against the yen and a one-week high against the euro before paring some of its gains in afternoon trading. The data heightened expectations that Friday's US non-farm payrolls report could deliver a positive surprise, said Jane Foley, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London. The euro was at $1.3165 from $1.3308 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG, down more than 1%. The dollar gained about 1.45% to trade at Y83.22 from Y82.05.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (bradley.davis@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1821 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Chile\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's $12 bln \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could have a meaningful effect on the exchange rate, but it all depends on the global backdrop, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts in a research note. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adds depreciation risks for the Chilean peso, but BoA \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  is holding onto its year-end forecast for USD/CLP at 490.00. In addition, bets are on that Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will pause its benchmark rate at its January monetary policy meeting, and will resume tightening after a month or two, ending 2011 with policy rates near 4.5%, the note said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-05-11 1539ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110105e715000dh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
