 threats of market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  helped push the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  away from recent highs. USD/BRL ended at 1.6715 from 1.6610 Tuesday, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telekurs\f28 \uc2 \f2  via Factset. The real failed to rally as lower commodity prices abroad set the tone for the session. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was also restrained by declarations by Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega late Tuesday that Brazil's government had "infinite" measures at its disposal to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in case a strong local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  threatened to hurt the country's trade balance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1922 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys are getting whacked across the board with most yields notching double-digit rise. ADP report fuels the selloff as investors turns to stocks and commodities. The benchmark 10-yr yield recently hits a session high of 3.469%, not far from the 3.568% set on Dec 16, the highest since May. In recent trade, the benchmark 10-year note is 31/32 lower to yield 3.460%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1919 GMT [Dow Jones] Total state government revenue dropped to $1.1 trillion in 2009, a decline of 31% from $1.6 trillion in 2008, according to the U.S. Census Bureau. Tax income dropped by 8.5%, the first year-to-year decline in tax revenue since 2002. Meanwhile, state government spending rose by 3%. Total federal grants to states increased 13% to $477.7B.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (romy.varghese@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1918 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is a touch weaker after USD got a further boost overnight and further moves lower look likely today, says ANZ Senior Dealer Alex Sinton; "Support will hold on any deeper moves into the 0.7500 territory during local time as direction is guided by the Australian Dollar moves." The pair was last trading at 0.7609 vs 0.7637 late Wednesday. Sinton adds the USD moved higher overnight after the U.S. December Automated Data Payroll release forecasted a very strong U.S. December non-farm payroll release on Friday. Sinton expects a range of 0.7587 to 0.7639. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1916 GMT [Dow Jones] President Obama will press Chinese President Hu Jintao on Beijing's policy on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when the two meet at the White House in two weeks, White House spokesman Robert Gibbs said. The US continues to believe that China must take steps to allow its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the yuan, to continue to appreciate, said Gibbs. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy is a long-standing bone of contention between the two countries. The US views the yuan as artifically undervalued, while China is reluctant to loosen its hold on the yuan for fear of costing millions of Chinese workers their jobs because a stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has made the country's export products too expensive in world markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (michael.crittenden@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1853 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar gained broadly against its rivals after encouraging US data painted a brightening picture of the economy. The dollar soared to a two-week high against the yen and a one-week high against the euro before paring some of its gains in afternoon trading. The data heightened expectations that Friday's US non-farm payrolls report could deliver a positive surprise, said Jane Foley, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London. The euro was at $1.3165 from $1.330