\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  Peso Weaker, As  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Set To Begin\b0\par\par\uc2 1,211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2011\par 13:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1219 GMT [Dow Jones] After losing nearly 5% versus the dollar Tuesday, the Chilean peso weakened further Wednesday morning as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was set to begin. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will increase its foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves by $12 billion this year, starting with daily purchases of $50 million on the local market between Jan. 5 and end Feb. 9. Prior to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announcement late Monday the peso was trading at a 32-month highs versus the dollar, prompting exporters to clamor for a lifeline as the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength eats away at the competitiveness of their products abroad. In recent trading the peso was weaker at CLP491.40 to the dollar, versus Tuesday's close of CLP487.80, according to data provided by Valor Futuro newswire. (anthony.esposito@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1215 GMT [Dow Jones] Brazil's government is likely to continue to jawbone the market if the real moves below BRL1.65 per dollar, but its options for halting the appreciation are limited, says RBS. "We should expect more verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with BRL below 1.65, but not meaningful \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures," it says in a note. There might be "few options remaining without crossing over to more heterodox measures," it says. President Dilma Rousseff seems to be expressing a more moderate \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy stance, it says. Rousseff wants to "spur the competitiveness of the economy in order to protect the private sector from excessive BRL strength, maybe as a tacit recognition on the limits on effectively altering the BRL trend." (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1214 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece/10-year bund spreads on Wednesday rise 10 bps to reach a notional historic high of 975 bps, but with no transactions taking place at that level. Trading is subdued with just EUR2 billion in trades on the local HDAT and European trading platform, traders saying that the spread reflects a rally in German bunds rather than a sell-off in GGBs. "There has been no trading in the 10-year bond so far today. The market is very quiet," says a local bond trader. "We saw a rally in the bunds today but the peripherals failed to follow." (alkman.granitsas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1142 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD has stabilized during the European session after falling below parity in Asia Wednesday. Citi says that move came as the retracement in commodities and worries about the economic impact of the Queensland floods weighed. AUD/USD now trades at 1.0003, the bank says there is chatter of stops around 0.9980. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1127 GMT [Dow Jones] The downtrend in EUR/SEK is embarking on another bear wave towards the key support cluster around the 8.8500 level, extending the long-term decline off the March 2009 peak at 11.7861. See Wednesday's Charting Europe column by Francis Bray. (francis.bray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1119 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro falls to the day's lows across the board, with stops triggered in EUR/USD and EUR/JPY, says a UK bank trader. EUR/USD slides to 1.3212, EUR/JPY 108.51, EUR/GBP 0.8483 and EUR/CHF 1.2570. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1112 GMT [Dow Jones] Poland's Monetary Policy Council, or MPC, is likely to raise key rate by 25 basis points, to 3.75%, on January 19, \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says, commenting on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor's warning that policy tightening will be delivered soon. "Overall, we expect the MPC to deliver tightening of 50-75 bps, raising the policy rate to 4.00%-4.25% by end of 2Q," BNP says. But adds "rate hikes will prove to be a policy mistake, given softening GDP growth and the lack of underlying inflationary pressure." BNPP adds "Policy tightening in early 2011 might need to be reversed as soon as 4Q or early 2012." (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1108 GMT [Dow Jones] Portugal's EUR500 million six-month Treasury bill sale Wednesday brings "no particular surprise," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  strategist Giuseppe Maraffino. As expected, Portugal issued the amount announced but with a huge concession of around 15 basis points versus Tuesday's close, he says. He adds that there was no particular interest before the auction and this has favored the cheapening of the t-bill on auction. "Overall, the auction was good as it was bid and they issued the amount announced but at a cost of huge concession in terms of pricing," Maraffino adds. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1103 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR forward premia is lower after the increase in the spot USD/INR rates and receiving interest on improved liquidity among banks, a senior trader with a privately-run bank says. The one-year forward premia is at 2.43 from 2.5250 late Tuesday. The trader adds, some profit-booking in premia had been spotted, while data showing a major reduction in banks' daily borrowing from the RBI helps forward premia ease. Banks borrowed INR370.70 billion from the RBI Wednesday, compared with a INR1 trillion daily average last week. "If the spot keeps inching up to trade between 45.50 and 45.70, we could see the forward premia move in a band of 2.40 to 2.60 this week," the trader says. The USD/INR is at 45.29. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1101 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD's latest attempt to recover runs out of steam above 1.3260 and the spot falls to the day's low of 1.3228. The next point of interest is likely to be the December 31 low of 1.3217. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1059 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are stable as debt auctions in Germany and Portugal go through smoothly. Germany sells EUR3.916 billion of the 10-year benchmark, following the last three German bond auctions that were undersubscribed. Portugal meanwhile sold EUR500 million of T-bills, with the average yield coming in at 3.686%, up quite a bit from 2.045% at the last auction, although that did take place in September. March bunds are up 0.22 at 126.33, and the 10-year Portuguese/German yield spread is up 4.5 bps on the day at 375.5 bps, but largely stable since the auction. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1058 GMT [Dow Jones] The pressure on the euro continues in mid-morning European trading Wednesday after Portugal sold 6-month treasury bills at a significantly higher yield than the last 6-month auction in September. Although the auction was well covered the high yield it had to pay failed to provide any impetus to the euro which continues to suffer against the dollar, pound and most notably against the Swedish krona. Meanwhile, the dollar gained some ground back against the pound after PMI construction data showed a contraction in December. USD/JPY is little changed at 82.10, EUR/USD is down at 1.3246, USD/GBP is at 1.5591. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com) [ 01-05-11 0719ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110105e7150007v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
