\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Pres:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Is Exceptional\b0\par\par\uc2 140 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Januar 2011\par 14:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, announced after the market closed on Monday, is an exceptional measure and as such gives it credibility, the bank's president Jose De Gregorio said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 With the peso trading at 32-month highs against the dollar in recent months and exporters demanding a lifeline as the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cuts into the competitiveness of their products, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will move to increase its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by $12 billion this year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is the largest since the peso's free float was adopted in the late 1990s, won't, however, affect Chile's long-term competitiveness, De Gregorio said.\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 01-04-11 0930ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110104e7140004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
