\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Steps Latin America is taking to counter hot money\b0\par\par\uc2 569 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Januar 2011\par 21:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 Jan 4 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become the latest Latin American country to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, taking aggressive measures to protect exporters.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have poured money into the region's attractive economies, shunning markets in developed countries still struggling to recover from the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Below are the main steps Latin American countries have taken to curb the strength of their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CHILE\b0 \highlight\uc2  ANNOUNCES LONG-AWAITED \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announces record $12 billion in dollar purchases during 2011. It stresses that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is exceptional, warns against "addiction" to it.\par \par \uc2 * Peso plunges <CLP=CL> from near three-year high after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcement.\par \par \uc2 * Chilean President Sebastian Pinera says no plans for capital controls.\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifts limits on pension funds' overseas investment.\par \par \uc2 * Bank slows pace of interest rate hikes, citing peso.\par \par \uc2 -- Story links: [ID:nN04206531] [ID:nN04221079]\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL BUYS DOLLARS, FIGHTS INFLOWS, LOOKS TO TAX BREAKS\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed daily dollar purchases soon after the financial crisis, recently buying as much as $1 billion a day via double auctions.\par \par \uc2 * Sovereign wealth fund authorized to buy dollars on spot market but has yet to act.\par \par \uc2 * Government triples tax on foreign purchases of bonds to 6 percent to curb inflows into fixed income market.\par \par \uc2 * Government increases tax on derivatives margins to dissuade short-term investors.\par \par \uc2 * Raises bank reserve requirements to cool a credit boom and ease the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  situation by paving the way for lower interest rates.\par \par \uc2 * Aggressive measures including targeted tariff increases and tax breaks in the works to address effects of strong real <BRBY>.\par \par \uc2 -- Story links: [ID:nN30113578] [ID:nN04214108] [ID:nN03188472]\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA INTRODUCES DOLLAR PURCHASES, STRUCTURAL MEASURES\par \par \uc2 * Government kept $1.5 billion abroad last year, which included $1.4 billion in government dividends from state oil firm \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * Plans to possibly hedge up to $3.7 billion in external debt service payments in 2011.\par \par \uc2 * Will only use external financing this year to meet outside obligations to ease pressure on peso <COP=>.\par \par \uc2 * Eliminated tax exemption on foreign loan interest\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying at least $20 million daily until at least March 15.\par \par \uc2 -- Story links: [ID:nN29291838] [ID:nN16108112] [ID:nN02213742]\par \par \uc2 PERU BUYS DOLLARS, ENCOURAGES INVESTMENT ABROAD\par \par \uc2 * Government trims average tariff to 3.4 percent from 5 percent, possibly resulting in increased imports and less appreciation pressure on the sol <PEN=PE>.\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys about $9 billion dollars on the spot market in 2010, equivalent to around 6 percent of GDP. The treasury also bought around $500 million.\par \par \uc2 * Banking regulator SBS drawing up rule to curb use of short-term derivatives called non-deliverable forwards (NDFs) to limit pressure on the sol.\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises deposit requirements on bank accounts, especially those tied to foreign loans, in a bid to limit speculation on the sol.\par \par \uc2 * Regulators allow pension funds to invest more abroad.\par \par \uc2 -- Story links: [ID:nN26131354] [ID:Nn31130618]\par \par \uc2 MEXICO ESCHEWS \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 , PESO SET TO OUTPERFORM\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying $600 million per month by selling dollar put options as a means to build up its reserves.\par \par \uc2 * Mexico has said capital controls do not work.\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso <MXN=> lagged gains in other markets, yet to firm back to pre-crisis levels.\par \par \uc2 * Strategists see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures across Latin America boosting attraction of free-floating Mexican peso.\par \par \uc2 -- Story links: [ID:nN10261374] [ID:nN15182353]\par \par \uc2 LATAM-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABN|D|E|RBN|M|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110104e7140013l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
